│ Runtime: Local                                                                                     │
│ Max loops: 60                                                                                      │
│ Task: OWASP A01 Broken Access Control and A07 Authentication Failures, combined because            │
│ authorization and authentication findings chain constantly. Covers IDOR, missing function-level    │
│ checks, dispatcher bypass, weak session handling, and credential reuse.                            │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Object and function level access                                                            │
│ Objective: Determine what can be reached without the right identity.                               │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. Request objects belonging to other identifiers and compare responses. Where a directory,      │
│ index or lookup endpoint hands out identifiers, enumerate them and then RETRIEVE THE RECORDS       │
│ THEMSELVES - that enumeration is possible is a finding, but the records are the loot. Record each  │
│ one in full, including fields whose purpose is not yet clear: an opaque or credential-shaped value │
│ sitting inside a record you can already read is frequently the input to a later step, and worth    │
│ more than the enumeration that found it.                                                           │
│   2. Compare anonymous, low-privilege and high-privilege views of the same endpoint. Differences   │
│ reveal missing checks; identical results for anonymous and authenticated mean the resource is      │
│ simply public - say so rather than claiming a bypass.                                              │
│   3. Test privileged functionality directly rather than through the UI, including administrative   │
│ routes discovered in client code or metadata.                                                      │
│   4. Exercise every HTTP method an endpoint accepts, not just the one the client uses.             │
│   5. Probe endpoints that dispatch, batch, proxy or import: reaching a handler indirectly          │
│ frequently bypasses checks applied to its direct route. Compare the direct and indirect result     │
│ explicitly.                                                                                        │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Authentication and session handling                                                         │
│ Objective: Assess how identity is established and maintained.                                      │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. Test for default, seeded or well-known credentials.                                           │
│   2. Examine the session representation: is it random, or does it encode or derive from something  │
│ predictable or recoverable?                                                                        │
│   3. Assess protections against automated guessing, but do NOT run volumetric guessing here - note │
│ the absence of a control rather than proving it by force.                                          │
│   4. If credential material is recoverable elsewhere in the assessment, test it here for reuse     │
│ across accounts and across every distinct authentication surface.                                  │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Prove and bound the impact                                                                  │
│ Objective: Convert access findings into demonstrated, bounded impact.                              │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. For every claimed bypass, run and report the control test: the same request without the       │
│ material or identity. A finding without a control result is not a finding.                         │
│   2. Establish breadth - one object or all of them, one account or every account. Breadth drives   │
│ severity more than technique.                                                                      │
│   3. Record what a compromised session actually grants: roles, privileged functions, other users'  │
│ data.                                                                                              │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Verify before reporting                                                                     │
│ Objective: Apply the same evidence standard to every finding, so results are comparable across     │
│ categories.                                                                                        │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. PROOF DISCIPLINE - label every finding DEMONSTRATED (you executed it and the response proves  │
│ it, quote that response), INFERRED (follows from evidence but was not executed), or UNVERIFIED     │
│ (suspected from a version, component or code pattern only). Identifying software is never a        │
│ demonstration; never write 'confirmed' without having executed something.                          │
│   2. RUN THE CONTROL TEST. Before claiming any access or bypass, repeat the request WITHOUT the    │
│ credential, token, header or material you believe is responsible. If the result is the same, you   │
│ have proven nothing - the resource was already reachable. State the control result alongside the   │
│ finding. This single omission is the most common source of false critical findings.                │
│   3. BUDGET DISCIPLINE - rank leads by evidence strength (material in hand > observed behaviour >  │
│ suspicion from a version string) and give each a step budget. STOP RULE: about 8-10 consecutive    │
│ steps on one lead with no new evidence means move on, recording what you tried.                    │
│   4. Record findings to notes as you go, with the endpoint, the request, the response and the      │
│ control result, so later phases and the report can use them.                                       │
│   5. Stay non-destructive: prefer read-only proof, do not modify or delete data, do not lock       │
│ accounts, and stop once the point is demonstrated.                                                 │
│                                                                                                    │
│                                                                                                    │
